+## 162.3 - (Tentatively Seemingly Fixed) Reproducible T259 `CvCity::getProductionBarPercentages+0x522` invalid-pointer crash exposed during AI military-production testing
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1QQGSLphz6DlglCALe_IdJNM0aiS8Ra8T?usp=sharing).
+
+While implementing and testing the KI#197 AI military-production changes, save file 454 produced a reproducible T259 crash in the existing production-bar crash family. Two dumps from the same situation both failed at `CvCity::getProductionBarPercentages+0x522`, with the same DLL-relative offset `0x479f2`, failure bucket/hash and invalid-pointer read instruction, while the actual invalid read addresses differed. This makes a deterministic fault in this callback much more likely than unrelated random Civ4 instability, but does not establish that the military-production changes themselves caused it.
+
+This case was particularly useful because KI#162.2 had previously crashed at `CvCity::getProductionBarPercentages+0x4e2`. The owner/city-registration hardening added afterward sits near the beginning of the callback, and the new crash is exactly `0x40` bytes later. Without matching line-symbol PDB information this does not prove that both offsets are the same generated instruction shifted by the added guard, but it strongly suggests that the earlier hardening did not address the underlying downstream failure.
+
+Review found a remaining structural weakness in `getProductionBarPercentages`: it validated the production queue head and its `OrderData` once, but then stopped using that validated snapshot and repeatedly re-read the city's current production state through helpers such as `isProductionProcess`, `getProductionNeeded`, `getCurrentProductionDifference` and `getProduction`. If nested/re-entrant work or any production-state change occurred between the validation and those later reads, the original validation no longer guaranteed that all bar calculations referred to the same order. The dumps alone cannot prove that such re-entry or queue mutation was the direct cause, so this remains the suspected mechanism rather than a confirmed root cause.
+
+The callback was therefore changed to keep one internally consistent validated production snapshot for the whole calculation:
+
+- Snapshot and validate the queue head's `eOrderType` and `iData1` once.
+- Return immediately for `ORDER_MAINTAIN`, which has no production bar.
+- Use the corresponding typed unit/building/project production-needed, stored-production and production-modifier accessors from that validated target instead of re-reading the generic current queue.
+- Calculate the bar rates through `getProductionDifference` from those snapshotted values rather than `getCurrentProductionDifference`.
+- Keep the existing invalid production-needed guard before any percentage calculation.
+
+This should preserve normal production-bar behavior while removing the inconsistent validate-one-order/read-another possibility from the callback. After applying this targeted change, the previously reproducible T259 crash no longer occurred in the confirming test. Because the exact machine instruction still cannot be resolved without matching line symbols and the proposed re-entrancy mechanism is not directly proven by the dump, this is recorded as **Seemingly Fixed** rather than a fully proven root-cause fix.
+
+Seemingly fixed with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.
+
