 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6025+) **Duplicate bonus value no longer over-counts reliable future-BFC coverage:** trade imports still do not count as owned as in Base AdvCiv, but bonuses already owned or plausibly claimed by one of our existing cities' future BFC no longer receive another full empire-wide duplicate value. Contested bonuses can still count when a known rival future BFC also threatens them, because ownership is not reliable. This prevents new cities from chasing a safe duplicate Crab (better go for bonuses we don't have to grow more) while still letting a contested Camel near Arabia matter (don't count on it empire-wide, but still locally there's a chance we might steal it so value it enough for local valuation only, not empire-wide one as not reliable enough for that). See [KI#178](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#178---fixedimproved-city-site-bonus-valuation-used-broad-trade-style-ai_bonusval-instead-of-settlement-specific-long-term-value).
 - **Settler site eval - devalue BFCs with many very bad tiles:** Candidate sites are **significantly devalued** (not auto-rejected) when enough non-home BFC plots are impassable or remain very low-yield after their best XML-valid improvement: the threshold as of now is dynamic `4 * food + 2 * production + commerce` (e.g., 1 food is not very bad, 1 hammer and 1 commerce is very bad, peak is very bad) (visible bonuses on usable plots are not counted because bonuses are likely to be always valuable). See [KI#26.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#262---enhanced-ai-settlers-not-devaluing-very-bad-non-home-tiles-peak-ice-cap-non-hill-desert-without-a-bonus-non-hill-snow-without-a-bonus-if-we-have-enough-of-them-in-bfc).
 - **First-city Good enough plots threshold**: AI settlers now also require the first-city site to have **enough** (e.g., >=13) **“good enough”** (i.e. tile is not impassable, not persistently barren by yield-potential logic, or is a reachable/already-improved water bonus) **BFC tiles** before accepting it, so the first-city is less likely to be founded on a long-term disadvantageous location.
-- **Respect water bonuses:** if a coast bonus would be in BFC but the site is non-coastal, apply a stronger penalty so we don’t "count" unusable sea resources. Penalty moved from **-165 → -400** (K-Mod used ~-800).
+- **Respect water bonuses:** if a coast bonus would be in BFC but the site is non-coastal, apply a stronger penalty so we don’t "count" unusable sea bonuses. Penalty moved from **-165 → -400** (K-Mod used ~-800).
 - **Low-food environments**: evaluate food scarcity from each non-home BFC plot's natural food and food added by bonus-specific improvements (e.g., tundra is low food, peak is very low food, but if there are bonuses that give food in the site it reduces the net food-scarcity score (e.g., high tundra high fish/deer may be better than grass/plain hybrid no food from bonuses so do not discard it)). When an inland site has too much net food scarcity, **prefer coastal sites** so the city can grow instead of remaining at very low population. See [KI#32](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#32---now-seemingly-fixed-prioritize-settling-on-coast-when-food-environment-is-low-many-tundra-orand-plains-orand-desert-orand-snow-orand-peak-although-i-assume-is-not-counted-but-to-be-safe-the-corinth-screenshot).
 - **Environment heuristics (non-bonus tiles):** favor sites with lots of grassland/flood plains; penalize plains/desert BFCs. For the tile you settle on, prefer hill-plains/tundra/desert; avoid settling on hill-grass/flood plains unless doing so unlocks a clearly superior multi-bonus BFC. See [KI#26](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#26---seemingly-fixed-for-the-plot-of-the-settled-city-only-in-general-the-ai-should-aim-to-settle-on-a-hill-and-avoid-settling-on-flatland-grassland-or-flood-plains-where-possible-or-during-the-first-city---city-1-when-there-is-no-good-site-yet-to-take).
 - **Health tolerance:** don’t reject good jungle sites just for early unhealth. Allow a threshold (more strict for capital, looser later), and consider forest health offsets and flood plains exceptions. Jungle can be chopped later; value the site’s potential, not just immediate health. (Threshold examples: ~≤350 "healthpercent" for capitals; ~≤450 for later cities.)
