 - **Remote captured-city attack stacks:** large AI attack **stacks in faraway captured cities no longer let upgrade waiting override a ready offensive target**. If the stack is large enough, has a valid target, and the target is not too strong, it keeps using the existing attack movement logic **instead of parking most of the empire's army far from its core** because several units could upgrade, which was very problematic. In the BBAI-log sample, the Celtic Boston trapped-stack pattern was visible from T156 to T256; the stricter remote-trap diagnostic confirmed it from T172 to T214, with up to 41 units / 62% of Celtic military parked in a faraway captured city while still having ready targets. See [KI#155](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#155---fixed-base-advciv-issue-remote-captured-city-attack-stacks-could-park-for-many-turns-because-upgrade-waiting-overrode-a-ready-offensive-target).
 - **Ready attack stacks stop parking for unavailable upgrades:** if a city-attack stack is ready, the target is not too strong, and **0 units can upgrade now**, the AI now keeps moving toward the target instead of waiting for possible future upgrades. This fixes real offensive freezes: in one BBAI-log sample, USA Washington waited from **T147-T181** with up to **59 units / 69% of total military** while targets were only **3-5 turns** away; Mali Gao waited from **T233-T255** with up to **42 units / 41% of total military**. The AI still waits for real immediate upgrades: a USA Seattle stack waited while **6 units could upgrade now**, then attacked/moved once that number became **0**. This is thought to be much stronger, because it uses the current military advantage before enemies catch up; old units can be spent in war instead of sitting around draining upkeep, needing future upgrade gold (solved if they die as well), and risking obsolescence; successful captures can then increase economy, unit support, and long-term growth; and the needless wait was very problematic and inefficient too. See [KI#156](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#156---fixed-base-advciv-issue-ready-city-attack-stacks-could-park-for-future-upgrades-even-when-no-unit-could-upgrade-now).
 - **AI unit upgrades - stop routine upgrades from draining the treasury:** Fixes an AI spending problem where normal unit **upgrades could overshoot the intended upgrade budget**. Previously, the AI only checked whether it was still below budget before starting the next upgrade, but did not check whether that next upgrade itself fit the remaining budget. For example, in BBAI diagnostics, an AI with 280 gold reserved for upgrades could spend 237 gold, still count as under budget, then start another 237 gold upgrade and end up spending 474 gold total, sometimes leaving only a few gold in the treasury. Routine **upgrades now check the actual remaining upgrade budget before upgrading**. **Emergency**-style upgrades, such as important city defenders or special impassable-unit upgrades, **remain more flexible**, but normal upgrades are much less likely to waste all available gold. AIs in **financial trouble also avoid** routine upgrades **unless they are focused on war**. See [KI#160](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#160---fixed-likely-inherited-ai-upgrade-budget-issue-normal-upgrades-could-overshoot-the-remaining-budget-and-leave-the-ai-almost-broke).
